1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,170
Alternatively, we can think, as most mathematicians do,

2
00:00:04,270 --> 00:00:08,364
that these formulas are merely descriptions of certain

3
00:00:08,464 --> 00:00:13,178
pre-existing mathematical structures, and what math really is,

4
00:00:13,278 --> 00:00:16,052
is the set of all of such structures.

5
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:18,219
But what would the set consisting

6
00:00:18,319 --> 00:00:20,852
of all mathematical structures look like?

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,951
It would have to be something very general and very large, such

8
00:00:25,051 --> 00:00:29,132
that we could simulate essentially whatever we want inside of it.

9
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:32,374
This is where set theory comes in. A set

10
00:00:32,474 --> 00:00:36,140
is perhaps the most basic object we can consider.

11
00:00:36,333 --> 00:00:40,012
I challenge you to try to define a set without

12
00:00:40,112 --> 00:00:44,777
using a synonym of set, like collection, group, class etc.

13
00:00:44,933 --> 00:00:49,921
The axioms of ZFC are a solid attempt to define what a set is.

14
00:00:50,066 --> 00:00:52,986
The origins of set theory date back to

15
00:00:53,086 --> 00:00:56,086
Georg Cantor in the later 19th century.

16
00:00:56,266 --> 00:00:58,995
Cantor was a mathematical platonist who

17
00:00:59,095 --> 00:01:02,766
envisioned an unrestricted and powerful mathematics.

